But again, we were strafed by the German Luftwaffe. They came, and just-- I-- to this day I can hear the bombs fall right and left, and the planes coming down and, you know, come in a dive, and with their machine guns, and just indiscriminately just shoot at everybody on the road.

Again, we escaped being hit. And eventually we made it to Reims-- I mean, to Rettel, where my uncle-- Dad's brother Charles-- lived. And he was also in the cattle and horse business. We told him, I says, you better pack your bags, come with us, because the Germans are just a couple of kilometers behind over there. They're going to be here by tomorrow morning for doggone sure.

He says, ah, no. The Maginot Line, the French Maginot Line will hold them. Don't worry about that. We, the French. He says, forget that. Just come with us. Well, they wouldn't want to go.

